1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,351
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:01,351 --> 00:00:10,129
This is '불국사' [bul-guk-sa] (Bulguksa) Temple in '경주' [gyeong-ju] (Gyeongju) — a beautiful temple famous as a World Heritage site.

3
00:00:10,129 --> 00:00:14,350
Shall we learn living, real Korean together today?

4
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:22,099
When you watch dramas or listen to '케이팝' [ke-i-pap] (K-pop),

5
00:00:22,099 --> 00:00:25,392
you hear real Korean that no textbook teaches.

6
00:00:25,392 --> 00:00:27,970
Those expressions are today's stars.

7
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:37,554
Today we'll learn '반말' [ban-mal] (casual speech) and '축약어' [chuk-yak-eo] (shortened words).

8
00:00:37,554 --> 00:00:41,550
Korean culture will feel much closer to you.

9
00:00:46,510 --> 00:00:48,133
Strolling through Bulguksa,

10
00:00:48,133 --> 00:00:50,476
alive with a thousand years of history,

11
00:00:50,476 --> 00:00:52,940
even tricky Korean becomes much more fun.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,900
Korean has one very important feature: the way you speak changes completely depending on who you're talking to.

13
00:01:03,390 --> 00:01:03,626
Now,

14
00:01:03,626 --> 00:01:07,347
let's begin today's lesson before these beautiful stone stairs.

15
00:01:07,347 --> 00:01:08,410
Are you all ready?

16
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:20,010
Korean speech splits into two: '존댓말' [jon-daet-mal] (polite speech) and '반말' [ban-mal] (casual speech).

17
00:01:24,280 --> 00:01:29,348
'존댓말' [jon-daet-mal] (polite speech) is for elders and people you just met.

18
00:01:29,348 --> 00:01:33,740
'반말' [ban-mal] (casual speech) is only for very close friends.

19
00:01:35,790 --> 00:01:43,492
The secret is simple: just drop the ending '요' [yo] (yo) or '습니다' [seup-ni-da] (seumnida),

20
00:01:43,492 --> 00:01:47,450
and it becomes casual. Let's compare!

21
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:54,262
First: '밥 먹었어요' [bap-meok-eot-eo-yo]? (Have you eaten?).

22
00:01:54,262 --> 00:02:00,320
Drop the '요' [yo], and to a friend it becomes '밥 먹었어' [bap-meok-eot-eo]?.

23
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:08,195
Did you hear it? Only the endings differ.

24
00:02:08,195 --> 00:02:10,109
Among close friends,

25
00:02:10,109 --> 00:02:14,130
we drop the '요' [yo] and casually ask '밥 먹었어' [bap-meok-eot-eo]?.

26
00:02:18,710 --> 00:02:23,432
Second: '어디 가요' [eo-di-ga-yo]? (Where are you going?).

27
00:02:23,432 --> 00:02:28,500
Among friends, drop the '요' [yo] and ask '어디 가' [eo-di-ga]?.

28
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:35,854
'어디 가요' [eo-di-ga-yo]? shortens to '어디 가' [eo-di-ga]?. The shorter it gets,

29
00:02:35,854 --> 00:02:40,120
the closer and more comfortable the relationship.

30
00:02:44,290 --> 00:02:47,016
Gratitude works the same.

31
00:02:47,016 --> 00:02:54,320
'고마워요' [go-ma-wo-yo] (Thank you) becomes '고마워' [go-ma-wo] to a friend when you drop the '요' [yo].

32
00:02:57,530 --> 00:02:58,885
The words shorten,

33
00:02:58,885 --> 00:03:01,370
but the gratitude stays the same.

34
00:03:01,370 --> 00:03:04,080
To a close friend, warmly say '고마워' [go-ma-wo].

35
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:09,743
Same when parting.

36
00:03:09,743 --> 00:03:17,390
'잘 가요' [jal-ga-yo] (Goodbye) becomes '잘 가' [jal-ga] to a friend without the '요' [yo].

37
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:22,728
All four differ only at the ending.

38
00:03:22,728 --> 00:03:25,328
Read aloud after the native speaker,

39
00:03:25,328 --> 00:03:28,940
and feel the presence or absence of the final '요' [yo].

40
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:38,134
But be careful: '반말' [ban-mal] (casual speech) feels warm among friends,

41
00:03:38,134 --> 00:03:41,700
yet sounds very rude to elders or strangers.

42
00:03:43,660 --> 00:03:46,637
Next, let's look at '축약어' [chuk-yak-eo] (abbreviation),

43
00:03:46,637 --> 00:03:51,720
the fun shortened words you often see in messages and on social media.

44
00:03:55,410 --> 00:03:57,969
'축약어' [chuk-yak-eo] (abbreviation) isn't standard,

45
00:03:57,969 --> 00:04:03,230
but knowing it helps you understand real Korean conversations much better.

46
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:07,167
Typing fast on a smartphone,

47
00:04:07,167 --> 00:04:08,923
people naturally shorten words.

48
00:04:08,923 --> 00:04:11,870
Let's compare the full words with their short forms.

49
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:20,015
'생일 축하해' [saeng-il-chuk-ha-hae] (Happy birthday) shortens to '생축' [saeng-chuk].

50
00:04:20,015 --> 00:04:23,140
Friends send this on birthdays.

51
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:29,383
See — just the first syllables make '생축' [saeng-chuk].

52
00:04:29,383 --> 00:04:34,270
Gathering each word's first letter is the basic rule of abbreviations.

53
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:40,263
Cafés use it too.

54
00:04:40,263 --> 00:04:44,013
'아이스 아메리카노' [a-i-seu-a-me-ri-ka-no] (iced americano) is long,

55
00:04:44,013 --> 00:04:47,560
so people often shorten it to '아아' [a-a].

56
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:56,243
Order '아아 주세요' [a-a-ju-se-yo] at a café and you'll sound truly Korean.

57
00:04:56,243 --> 00:04:58,240
The hot one is '뜨아' [tteu-a]!

58
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:08,009
How about '진짜 재미있다' [jin-jja-jae-mi-it-da] (really fun)?

59
00:05:08,009 --> 00:05:15,150
Sweet '꿀' [kkul] (honey) meets '재미' [jae-mi] (fun) to make '꿀잼' [kkul-jaem].

60
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:21,511
The opposite, not fun at all,

61
00:05:21,511 --> 00:05:25,587
is '노잼' [no-jaem] — English 'no' plus '재미' [jae-mi] (fun).

62
00:05:25,587 --> 00:05:27,050
Clever, right?

63
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:36,132
Slang is warm among friends,

64
00:05:36,132 --> 00:05:39,022
but avoid it in formal settings or before elders.

65
00:05:39,022 --> 00:05:41,440
Choose the right words for the situation.

66
00:05:44,530 --> 00:05:50,600
Now let's talk about smart ways to study Korean with '케이팝' [ke-i-pap] (K-pop) and K-drama.

67
00:05:55,160 --> 00:06:01,830
It's a waste just to enjoy your favorite songs and dramas — they're the best material for learning pronunciation and intonation.

68
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:05,392
Read your favorite lyrics aloud,

69
00:06:05,392 --> 00:06:06,497
one line at a time.

70
00:06:06,497 --> 00:06:09,580
This helps enormously with fixing your pronunciation.

71
00:06:11,590 --> 00:06:12,463
With dramas,

72
00:06:12,463 --> 00:06:16,902
pause on a line and shadow the subtitle sentence by sentence.

73
00:06:16,902 --> 00:06:20,540
This '섀도잉' [syae-do-ing] (shadowing) practice is very effective.

74
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:33,380
Now let's practice a real phone call with a close friend using the casual speech we learned — here at the peaceful '석굴암' [seok-gul-am] (Seokguram).

75
00:06:36,250 --> 00:06:36,593
First,

76
00:06:36,593 --> 00:06:39,850
listen carefully to the casual question your friend asks.

77
00:06:43,130 --> 00:06:44,890
The question: '주말에 뭐 해' [ju-mal-e-mwo-hae]?

78
00:06:44,890 --> 00:06:47,310
(What are you doing this weekend?

79
00:06:47,310 --> 00:06:51,050
) — a very natural casual question between friends.

80
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:57,328
'주말에 뭐 해요' [ju-mal-e-mwo-hae-yo]? drops the '요' [yo] to become '주말에 뭐 해' [ju-mal-e-mwo-hae]?.

81
00:06:57,328 --> 00:07:02,660
Lowering the ending gives that casual feel.

82
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:09,421
Answer like this: '그냥 집에서 케이드라마 봐. 너는'?

83
00:07:09,421 --> 00:07:13,382
(Just watching K-dramas at home. You?).

84
00:07:13,382 --> 00:07:16,550
The casual '너는' [neo-neun]? feels friendly.

85
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,693
Now shadow '그냥 집에서 케이드라마 봐. 너는'?

86
00:07:19,693 --> 00:07:25,500
with natural pronunciation and intonation — like a real conversation!

87
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:31,862
Today we compared '존댓말' [jon-daet-mal] and '반말' [ban-mal] one by one,

88
00:07:31,862 --> 00:07:34,900
and learned everyday slang too.

89
00:07:34,900 --> 00:07:36,860
Quite a full lesson!

90
00:07:37,310 --> 00:07:39,202
Read real-life sentences aloud,

91
00:07:39,202 --> 00:07:40,605
clearly and repeatedly.

92
00:07:40,605 --> 00:07:43,290
Your Korean reading speed will grow quickly.

93
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:48,553
Practice with real conversation sentences,

94
00:07:48,553 --> 00:07:49,973
not just isolated letters,

95
00:07:49,973 --> 00:07:52,430
and Korean will soon feel much more familiar.

96
00:07:54,530 --> 00:07:58,093
I recommend using today's expressions at least once a day.

97
00:07:58,093 --> 00:08:00,120
Small practice builds big skills.

98
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:05,449
Studying Korean in Gyeongju,

99
00:08:05,449 --> 00:08:07,177
a thousand-year-old capital,

100
00:08:07,177 --> 00:08:09,770
makes the learning feel wonderfully vivid.

101
00:08:10,650 --> 00:08:13,181
Master standard '존댓말' [jon-daet-mal] first,

102
00:08:13,181 --> 00:08:19,510
then gradually mix in '반말' [ban-mal] with close friends — that's the best order.

103
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:20,533
Next time,

104
00:08:20,533 --> 00:08:24,486
I'll meet you again at another beautiful Korean landmark with useful,

105
00:08:24,486 --> 00:08:25,460
real-life Korean.

106
00:08:27,350 --> 00:08:31,590
Subscribing and liking give great strength to making richer Korean lessons.

107
00:08:31,590 --> 00:08:33,060
Great job today — goodbye!

